 in the 1970s so in  the 1970s there were two not one but two  oil price shocks and after the first one  which is after the Yom Kippur War  1973-74 oil prices actually stayed high  and then we had a second shock in that  decade in 1979 with the Iran Iraq war so  the reason I'm pointing this out is if  you needed to get a sense of how the  cost of living your cost of living is  going to be impacted then it's a level  and not just the percentage that we need  to be mindful of and I think the second  thing that I would say about a indicator  you should look for is we often see  headline inflation so this is CPI  Consumer Price Index  there is another measure called core  inflation so this is a measure of  inflation that strips out volatile items  like food and energy because I mean look  at how quickly Energy prices have  fluctuated and how quickly food prices  have fluctuated because unlike the 1970s  the middle which was energy shocks in  the Middle East Russia and Ukraine are  also major exporters of hard and soft  Commodities so things metals like  Palladium food fertilizer wheat so this  means that those are adding to the  inflationary pressures but core  inflation says don't look at food and  energy what is inflation when you strip  those things out so core inflation is a  measure that's trying to pick up how  much of this inflation has become  embedded in the way that firms produce  so core inflation is running at around  six percent and that's very high that's  about three times the bank of England  Target so that measure I think will give  a sense of whether inflation is here to  stay how embedded it is core inflation  can also fluctuate  um so here's my sort of final analogy  for the 1970s the end of the 1970s and  the huge inflation of that period  resulted in a global recession in the  early 1980s  increases and interest rates by Paul  Walker at the U.S fed Central Bank  Governor then he controlled inflation by  raising interest rates the cost of  borrowing so recession in a sense  brought down inflation so  how depressed are we now I'm telling you  we could end up with uh with and and you  know and the the forecasts are we are  headed for Global recession next year  and the UK is probably in recession now  that's at least what the chancellor has  said so but what that does for inflation  is it reduces demand and actually brings  uh price pressures down but this is  what's what's known as sort of known as  the misery index like unemployment  recession or inflation so it is a it is  a it is a turbulent  um time I don't want to  um understate that but I always hope we  can learn lessons from history so finish  with some thoughts on lessons from  history so I mentioned there what  monetary policy uh did monetary policy  operates a bit differently now but I  think what we need to uh hope the lesson  has been learned is monetary policy  needs to hit essentially a neutral level  where it's at the rate that doesn't  depress the economy and doesn't  stimulate more inflation because 80 of  this inflation is imported so it's it's  um it's Global factors  um so monetary policy can act but it  needs to act in that very it's a very  difficult range to hit because what is  the neutral rate of interest you know  before the banking crisis it was about  five percent was on average what the  interest rate was  um we're just not sure what it is now  because growth rate is lower interest  rate might be lower if inflation is very  high do we need to consider that so all  of that makes it quite hard to calibrate  where monetary policy should be I think  this period is closest to the 1970s so  it is unusual but as Mark Twain  reportedly said history doesn't repeat  itself but it does Rhyme so so we do not  have a there are lessons from the 70s  but there are differences as well and I  think one of the  um the main differences is uh very  different ways in which central banks  operate  um and different causes of this of this  inflation so  unfortunately though because these are  supply side shocks as in it's a shock of  energy prices and food prices that  drives our production costs  um there are limited ways that Economic  Policy can try and cushion this so if  you think about a normal risk not  there's no normal recession but a  recession where demand collapses you can  use fiscal policy to support people's  incomes and because prices are down  output is down you can see how it all  works together the problem now is when  you have a 1970 scenario which is like  stagflation High inflation stagnant  economies and recession  prices and output are moving in opposite  directions so you want to support people  who are vulnerable by giving them income  support that pushes up their demand and  that can be inflationary if the if it's  widespread support and it's done on the  basis of government borrowing so pushing  of demand is inflationary when inflation  is the problem and this is why the 1970  stagflation scenario is is challenging  to to deal with in the 1970s monetary  policy what central banks do is very  focused on targeting monetary Aggregates  the money supply didn't work too well  especially in the 80s money is a very  complicated thing as we will discuss in  just a sec and then the UK moved to a  European exchange rate mechanism a fixed  exchange rate and that essentially  dictated a lot of what money the policy  would be so we crashed out of the ERM in  1992 and launched a period of searching  for a new aim and it became inflation  targeting so expectations having a very  clear Target of two percent does mean  that you have more of a sense of where  central banks are headed where the bank  of England is looking for and so I think  that's one way and credibility in  meeting that Target is important now the  bank hasn't always been successful in  meeting that but at least you you get  that letter from the governor to the  chancellor explaining why but that the  regime itself is a lot more transparent  than what we had before and I think that  increases  helps shape Market expectations and that  could help us now because this is a time  in which you do want to know where the  central bank is going to do and not just  think oh you know with a less  transparent regime so actually Vince  cable is in the house everyone  foreign  we're here to talk about the future of  money so if I may maybe if I could um  just ask you  um to to look ahead a bit I've sort of  covered off where I thought um current  inflation and the cost of living crisis  should be but if we look ahead there's  some really interesting Trends and your  your book money and power obviously  takes a a huge a look at the big picture  I recommend all of you pick up a copy of  it but just talk us through what you  think the future of money is headed is  it cashless will there be crypto will  there be things about policy regimes be  great to get your take so to continue  watching this video click the link in  the top left or in the description below  or visit iai.tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to The Institute of Arts  and ideas at IAI TV

